// @emit-error nested arrays are not supported
// STILL A REJECT, AND THE REJECT IS THE IMPROVEMENT. An un-annotated NESTED literal whose
// INNER element is a value union (`[[1, "a"]]`) was `vl check`-clean INVALID WASM on the
// parent — `expected i32, found (ref $type)` at instantiation. It is now a loud emit
// reject, and this file pins that, because trading a silent miscompile for a diagnostic is
// the whole shape of the change it came out of (`inferred-union-element-literal.vl`).
//
// THE MECHANISM, and why the reject is the right stopping point. `arrLitIsRef`'s nested
// rung claims an outer literal only when the INNER one is NOT itself a ref list:
//
//     if e0 is ArrayLit { if !arrLitIsRef(a.arrElems[0]) { return true } }
//
// Registering the inner literal's union makes the inner a ref list (kind 2), so the outer
// stops being claimed and falls to the i32 list, where the inner list's ref hits that
// path's floor. That is exactly the class the rung's own header already excludes — "a REF
// inner literal (`[[{v: 1}]]`) stays annotation-driven" — so this literal did not acquire a
// new limitation, it JOINED an existing one, and the sibling `[[s]]` over a declared struct
// rejects identically on the parent and on this build.
//
// SUPPORTING IT IS A REAL DESIGN STEP, not a wider guard. A nested element's kind is the
// INNER list's wrapper — 4 / 6 / 7 / 8 for an i32-ish / string / f64 / i64 leaf — and a box
// list has no such kind; `tyNestedArrLeafSupported`, the shared gate that decides both
// whether the `<elem>[]` annotation is synthesized and whether the row is interned, admits
// only those five leaves for the reasons its own header sets out. A union leaf needs a
// ref-list-of-ref-lists element kind and an inner row interned at a strictly lower slot.
//
// THE ANNOTATED SPELLING ALREADY RUNS, on the parent and here, which is what makes this a
// synthesis gap rather than a missing rep:
//     const nested: (i32 | string)[][] = [[1, "a"]]   // prints 1 then a
//
// TOP-LEVEL ON PURPOSE. Wrapped in a function the same program reports `unsupported for-in
// iterable` instead — the outer `for`-in over a list the emitter never typed fails first,
// and the array-literal floor is never reached. The diagnostic this file exists to pin is
// the one the literal itself raises, so the literal is where the program stops.
const nested = [[1, "a"]]
for inner in nested {
  for v in inner {
    if v is i32 { print(v) } else { print(v) }
  }
}
